      "shortDescription": "Reading the clock: hours, minutes, half past, and AM/PM.",
      "formation": "Number + 時[じ] (o'clock) ＋ Number + 分[ふん] / 分[ぷん] (minutes)",
      "explanation": "To tell time, attach 時[じ] to a number for the hour and 分[ふん] for the minutes. The numbers on the clock are your first big set of kanji — learn 一[いち] through 十二[じゅうに] here, because you'll read them constantly. The hours are mostly regular, but three break the pattern and must be memorized:\n- 一時[いちじ] (1:00)\n- 二時[にじ] (2:00)\n- 三時[さんじ] (3:00)\n- 四時[よじ] (4:00) — よ, never し\n- 五時[ごじ] (5:00)\n- 六時[ろくじ] (6:00)\n- 七時[しちじ] (7:00) — しち, not なな\n- 八時[はちじ] (8:00)\n- 九時[くじ] (9:00) — く, not きゅう\n- 十時[じゅうじ] (10:00)\n- 十一時[じゅういちじ] (11:00)\n- 十二時[じゅうにじ] (12:00)\n\nMinutes use 分, which becomes ぷん after some numbers: 一分[いっぷん], 三分[さんぷん], 六分[ろっぷん], 八分[はっぷん], 十分[じゅっぷん]. For a half hour, use 半[はん]: 三時半[さんじはん] (3:30). Japan uses both 12- and 24-hour clocks; to be clear in the 12-hour system, put 午前[ごぜん] (AM) or 午後[ごご] (PM) *before* the time — 午後[ごご]三時[さんじ] (3 PM). Ask the time with 何時[なんじ] (what time) or 何分[なんぷん] (how many minutes): 今[いま]何時[なんじ]ですか (What time is it now?).",
      "rules": [
        "〜時[じ] = the hour (o'clock); 〜分[ふん] / 分[ぷん] = minutes",
        "Irregular hours: 四時[よじ] (4), 七時[しちじ] (7), 九時[くじ] (9)",
        "半[はん] = half past: 三時半[さんじはん] = 3:30",
        "午前[ごぜん] = AM, 午後[ごご] = PM — placed before the time",
        "Ask with 何時[なんじ] (what time) / 何分[なんぷん] (how many minutes)"
      ],
