    {
      "id": "years-when",
      "name": "Years & Scheduling — 〜年[ねん] and に",
      "shortDescription": "Saying the year, relative times, and marking when things happen.",
      "formation": "Number + 年[ねん] (year) ； specific time + に (at / on / in)",
      "explanation": "Years attach 年[ねん] to a number: 2024年[ねん] (にせんにじゅうよねん). Ask which year with 何年[なんねん]. Relative years have their own words — 今年[ことし] (this year), 来年[らいねん] (next year), 去年[きょねん] (last year) — and the same 今／来／先 pattern runs through weeks and months:\n- 今週[こんしゅう] / 来週[らいしゅう] / 先週[せんしゅう] (this / next / last week)\n- 今月[こんげつ] / 来月[らいげつ] / 先月[せんげつ] (this / next / last month)\n- 毎[まい]〜 means \"every\": 毎日[まいにち], 毎週[まいしゅう], 毎年[まいとし]\n\nThe most important rule of the chapter is when to use the particle に for \"at / on / in.\" Use に with *specific, absolute* time points — clock times, dates, days of the week, months, and years:\n- 三時[さんじ]に (at 3:00), 月曜日[げつようび]に (on Monday), 五月[ごがつ]に (in May)\n\nBut do NOT use に with *relative* time words — today, tomorrow, this week, every day — because they're defined relative to now, not fixed on the calendar:\n- 今日[きょう], 明日[あした], 昨日[きのう], 毎日[まいにち], 来週[らいしゅう], 今年[ことし] — no に\n\nSo you say 月曜日[げつようび]に行[い]きます (I'll go on Monday) but 明日[あした]行[い]きます (I'll go tomorrow).",
      "rules": [
        "〜年[ねん] = year (2024年[ねん]); ask with 何年[なんねん]",
        "Use に with specific times: 三時[さんじ]に, 月曜日[げつようび]に, 五月五日[ごがついつか]に",
        "Do NOT use に with relative words: 今日[きょう], 明日[あした], 毎日[まいにち], 来週[らいしゅう]",
        "今[こん]／来[らい]／先[せん] + 週[しゅう]／月[げつ] = this / next / last week or month",
        "毎[まい]〜 = every: 毎日[まいにち], 毎週[まいしゅう], 毎年[まいとし]"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "japanese": "来年[らいねん]日本[にほん]に行[い]きます。",
          "romaji": "Rainen Nihon ni ikimasu.",
          "english": "I'll go to Japan next year. (no に on 来年)"
        },
        {
          "japanese": "月曜日[げつようび]に会議[かいぎ]があります。",
